            "arguments": "{\"name\": \"reader-tester\", \"agent_type\": \"general-purpose\", \"mode\": \"sync\", \"description\": \"Reader comprehension test of doc draft\", \"prompt\": \"You are a reader-comprehension tester, not a style/technical reviewer.\\n\\nContext: A reader is an Elastic Observability user configuring OpenTelemetry (OTel) metric ingestion into Elastic. Their goal: understand whether histogram and counter metrics with cumulative temporality are supported, and what they need to do to use them, depending on how they send data to Elastic (via the Elastic Elasticsearch OTLP endpoint vs. via the OpenTelemetry Collector's Elasticsearch exporter).\\n\\nHere is the complete content of the relevant section (this is the ONLY content you may use to answer questions — do not use outside knowledge):\\n\\n---\\n## Metrics data ingestion\\n\\n### Histogram and counter temporality\\n\\n[applies_to: stack ga 9.5, serverless ga]\\n\\nWhen you send data using the Elasticsearch OTLP endpoint (`motlp`), both `delta` and `cumulative` temporality are supported for histograms and counters. Refer to \\\"Metric temporality\\\" (linked doc) for details, including the requirement to set `xpack.otel_data.histogram_field_type` to `exponential_histogram` for cumulative histograms. This setting is the default from stack version 9.4.\\n\\n[applies_to: stack unavailable, serverless unavailable]\\n\\nWhen you send data through the collector's Elasticsearch exporter, ingestion of OpenTelemetry metrics with the type `Histogram` is only supported with `delta` temporality. Histograms with `cumulative` temporality are dropped before being ingested into Elasticsearch. Make sure to export histogram metrics with delta temporality or use the `cumulativetodelta processor` as a workaround to convert the temporality for histogram metrics.\\n\\n### Exemplars\\n\\nExemplars are discarded when ingesting metrics. Support for exemplars is planned for a future release.\\n---\\n\\nTask: Predict 3-5 realistic questions this reader would have, answer each using ONLY the supplied text, and flag any BLOCKING ambiguity, missing prerequisite, contradiction, assumed knowledge, or unclear next action (a \\\"blocking\\\" issue is one that would stop the reader from succeeding or cause them to misconfigure something). Do not comment on style, syntax, technical accuracy, or applies_to tag correctness — only comprehension. Report clearly which issues (if any) are blocking vs. optional/nice-to-have.\"}"
